{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 
\par }\pard\plain \s1\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\cf6\lang3081\cgrid {Calculate Primary Directions
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 
\par To open the 'Primary Directions' window, click Calculate > Primary Directions from the main screen menu.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {Introduction
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 Use this utility to calculate a list of primary directions.
\par 
\par Primary directions is one of the most ancient methods of forecasting and is based on planetary movement due to the rotation of the earth on its own axis.
\par 
\par Primary Directions are also known as Primary Arcs, Equatorial Arcs, and Placidean Arcs.
\par 
\par The promittor (the planet or point being moved due to the rotation of the earth) is directed to the position of the significator (the fixed planet or point) and the direction arc is converted to a date.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {How to Calculate Primary Directions
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 
\par }{\f1\cf2 =>  Select From Age and To Age.
\par }\pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {Range 0 to 350 years.
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 This extended range has been provided to handle historical charts.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf2 => Click the Planets button to open the Planets and Extra Points dialog box.
\par }\pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {Promittor planets and points can be selected independently to Significator planets and points.
\par 
\par A 'traditional' planet selection might be:
\par Promittors: Sun, Moon, Ascendant, Midheaven, Part of Fortune, PreNatal New Moon and the PreNatal Full Moon.
\par Significators: Sun through to Saturn, Moon's North and South Nodes.
\par 
\par A 'modern' planet selection might be:
\par Promittors: Sun through to Pluto, Ascendant and Midheaven.
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 Significators: Sun through to Pluto, Ascendant and Midheaven.
\par 
\par Extra points can be found at the end of the promittor planets list and the significator planets list.
\par 
\par The Extra points are:
\par * PreNatal New Moon - also known as the degree of conjunction.
\par * PreNatal Full Moon - also known as the degree of prevention.
\par * Part of Faith
\par * Part of Spirit
\par * Part of Hyleg
\par * Part of Substance
\par * Custom Point 
\par 
\par The Part of Fortune calculation options can be found in the Options dialog box.
\par The custom point can be defined in the Options dialog box.
\par The Part of Substance calculation option can be found in the Options dialog box.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf2 => Click the Stars button to open the Stars dialog box.
\par }\pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {Promittor stars can be selected independently to Significator stars.
\par Tick the Include Selected Stars check box to use selected stars otherwise selected stars will be ignored.
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 Tick the Only Show Conjunctions to Stars check box to get conjunctions otherwise aspects from the selected aspect file will be used.
\par An * will appear beside those stars which are brighter than selected magnitude and also fall within the selected latitude limit. Use this feature to identify bright stars close to the ecliptic.
\par Click the Select Bright Stars button to select only the  * stars and de-select all others.
\par Click the Select All button to turn on all stars.
\par Click the Clear button to turn all stars off.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf2 => Click the Aspects button to open the Aspects dialog box.
\par }\pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {Select the aspects you wish to use - the Ptolemy aspects Conjunct, Opposition, Trine, Square and Sextile are normally used.
\par Parallels and contra-parallels can also be selected.
\par Tick the Conjunct check box if you only want conjunctions.
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 Only aspect names and angles are used. Aspect orbs are not used here.
\par Custom aspects can be defined via the main screen menu under Edit > Custom Aspects.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf2 => Click the Options button to open the Options dialog box.
\par }{\f1 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Format
\par }{\f1 Normally Rich Text, Symbols and Color are normally ticked.
\par Rich Text is good for copying and pasting the list of directions into a document.
\par Plain Text is best for copying and pasting the list of directions into an email.
\par Tick the Decimal Arc check box if you wish to display the direction arc as a decimal number, otherwise degrees, minutes and seconds will be used.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Features
\par }{\f1 The Auto Scale Font Size check box is normally ticked.
\par When the check box is ticked the selected font size will automatically reduce in small steps to avoid text wrap. 
\par When the check box is clear the selected font size will be used regardless of the text length or size of the results text box.
\par 
\par The Add Time Column check-box is normally clear.
\par The time column will show when the aspect becomes exact using the time zone of the natal chart. This feature has been provided for research purposes.
\par 
\par The Direct Points to their own aspects check box is normally clear.
\par This feature is provided to assist in research projects.
\par Ticking this check box will produce directions like Sun D => dex Sextile Sun.
\par 
\par The Ignore aspects between Promittor Stars and Significator Stars check box is normally ticked.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Define Custom key
\par }{\f1 Select the value of the key expressed in minutes, seconds and 100th's of a second in RA.
\par The coefficient of the key is displayed to the right.
\par To use Cardan's key you would select 59 minutes and 12 seconds giving a coefficient of 1.0135135
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Define how the Part of Fortune is to be calculated.
\par }{\f1 You can select
\par * Use Day formula for a diurnal chart and use Night formula for a nocturnal chart.
\par * Use same formula for diurnal and nocturnal charts.
\par * The latitude to use.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Define how the Part of Substance is to be calculated.
\par }{\f1 The second house cusp ruler can be calculated using traditional planets or modern planets.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Define Custom Point
\par }{\f1 Enter the custom point name.
\par Select the point's ecliptical longitude in degrees, minutes, seconds and zodiac sign.
\par Select the point's ecliptical latitude in degrees, minutes, seconds and North or South.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf2 => Click the Sort button to open the Sort By dialog box.
\par }\pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {The sort options are:
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 * Date - normally selected.
\par * Promittor, Date
\par * Significator, Date
\par * Aspect, Date
\par * Promittor, Aspect, Date
\par * Significator, Aspect, Date
\par * Arc
\par * Promittor, Arc
\par * Significator, Arc
\par * Aspect, Arc
\par * Promittor, Aspect, Arc
\par * Significator, Aspect, Arc
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf2 => Select the desired font size from the drop-down list box.
\par }\pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {The font size behavior will depend on the setting of the Auto Scale Font Size check-box.
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 
\par }{\f1\cf2 => Select Method - normally Zodiac Placidus Semi-Arc
\par }{\f1 Methods are:
\par * Zodiac Placidus Semi-Arc
\par * Zodiac Regiomontanus under the pole
\par * Both zodiac methods.
\par * Mundane Placidus Semi-Arc
\par * Mundane Regiomontanus under the pole
\par * Both mundane methods
\par \tab 
\par }{\f1\cf2 => Select Direction - normally both.
\par }{\f1 Options are:
\par * Direct (clockwise)
\par * Converse (counter-clockwise)
\par * Both - normally selected
\par Traditionally, direct and converse directions are given equal importance.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf2 => Select Aspect Position - normally both.
\par }{\f1 Options are:
\par * Aspects of Promittor to Significator.
\par * Promittor to Aspects of Significator.
\par * Both.
\par 
\par When the Mundane Placidus Semi-Arc or the Mundane Regiomontanus method is selected, the Aspect Position frame is greyed out. These methods only calculate Promittor to aspects of the Significator.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf2 => Select one or more timing Keys - normally Ptolemy.
\par }\pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {Each key determines how the direction arc is converted to a date.
\par 
\par Options are:
\par * Ptolemy
\par The key will be listed as Pto.
\par This timing method is based on the principle of one Degree for a Year.
\par This method equates 1 degree of RA to 1 year of life.
\par One minute of RA equates to 6 days 5 hours of life.
\par 
\par * Naibod
\par The key will be listed as Nai.
\par This timing method is based on the Mean Solar Arc.
\par It uses the average motion of the Sun in RA.
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 The naibod rate of 59 minutes 8.33 seconds of arc in RA is equated to 1 year of life.
\par One degree of RA equates to 1 year 5 days 8 hours of life.
\par One minute of RA equates to 6 days 4 hours of life.
\par 
\par * Kepler (also known as Simmonite's method)
\par The key will be listed as Kep.
\par This timing method is based on the Birthday Arc.
\par This method calculates how far the Natal Sun travels in RA from the birthday to one day later.
\par 
\par * Placidus
\par This key will be listed as Pla.
\par This timing method is based on the True Solar Arc.
\par This method calculates the number of days after the birthday the Sun takes to travel the same distance in RA as the direction arc. The number of days represents the number of years of life. 
\par For example
\par 5.5 days = 5.5years
\par 5 days 12 hours = 5 years 6 months
\par 
\par * Custom
\par The custom rate can be defined in the Options dialog box.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf2 => Select one or more Latitude options.
\par }{\f1 Normally Promittor zero Latitude and Significator zero latitude.
\par }{\f1\cf1 
\par These 
options are only applicable for the Zodiac Placidus Semi-Arc and the Zodiac Regiomontanus methods, and not the Mundane Placidus Semi-Arc method or the Mundane Regiomontanus method where the promittor uses its own latitude and the significator uses its own
 latitude.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Standard options are:
\par }\pard\plain \s17\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs20\cf1\lang3081\cgrid {* Prom 0L -> Sig 0L means the promittor uses zero latitude and the significator uses zero latitude.
\par }\pard\plain \s18\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs20\cf1\lang3081\cgrid {* Prom PL -> Sig 0L means the promittor uses its own latitude and significator uses zero latitude.
\par * Prom 0L -> Sig SL means promittor uses zero latitude and significator uses its own latitude.
\par * Prom PL -> Sig SL means promittor uses its own latitude and significator uses its own latitude.
\par * Bianchini means use Bianchini's Latitude Circle to calculate the aspect point's latitude.
\par This option can only be selected when the Prom PL -> Sig SL option is selected.
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1\cf1 
\par Click the small Experimental Options button for more options. 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Experimental options are:
\par }\pard\plain \s18\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs20\cf1\lang3081\cgrid {* Prom SL -> Sig 0L means the promittor uses the significators latitude and the significator uses zero latitude.
\par * Prom 0L -> Sig PL means the promittor uses zero latitude and the significator uses the promittors latitude.
\par * Prom PL -> Sig PL means the promittor uses its own latitude and the significator uses the promittors latitude.
\par * Prom SL -> Sig SL means the promittor uses the significators latitude and the significator uses its own latitude.
\par * Prom SL -> Sig PL means the promittor uses the significators latitude and the significator uses the promittors latitude.
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1\cf1 The experimental options have been provided to assist in research projects.
\par }{\f1 
\par }{\f1\cf2 => Click the Calculate Directions button to generate a list of direction arcs and dates.
\par }\pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {The progress dialog box will appear.
\par Click the Stop button to stop calculations.
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {To print the primary directions list
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 Click File > Print to open the Print Options dialog box.
\par In most cases the printout will be in portrait paper orientation.
\par If stars are turned on, the promittors and significators will appear as words and the printout will be in landscape.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {To copy the primary directions list
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 Click Edit > Copy.
\par If you want to paste the list into a Word document using planet symbols, use rich text.
\par If you want to paste the list into an email, use plain text.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {Results Sequence
\par }{\b0\cf1 Z for zodiac or M for mundane
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 P for placidus semi-arc or R for regiomontanus under the pole
\par sin for sinister or dex for dexter
\par Aspect
\par Promittor
\par Latitude used by the promittor
\par D for direct or C for converse direction
\par => means directed to
\par sin or dex
\par Aspect
\par Significator
\par Latitude used by the significator
\par Direction Arc in RA
\par Key
\par Date
\par Time (optional)
\par 
\par The aspect only applies to the promittor or the significator, not both.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {
\par Promittor
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 The promittor is the planet or point that is being directed. It is said to promise an event based on its local determin
ation, which is the natal condition by sign, house position, house rulership etc.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {Significator
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 The significator is the planet or point that the promittor is directed to. As with the promittor, its local determination has to be taken into account when inte
rpreting the event that it signifies.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {
\par Sinister
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 Means the aspect is added counter-clockwise in the order of the zodiac signs.
\par For example let the Sun's position be 0 Aries. The aspect point of sinister sextile Sun would be 0 Gemini.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {Dexter
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 Means the aspect is added clockwise against the order of the zodiac signs.
\par For example let the Sun's position be 0 Aries. The aspect point of dexter sextile Sun would be 0 Aquarius.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {Zodiac Parallels
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 A planet's longitude and latitude is first used to calculate its declination.
\par The parallel position of the planet is that point on the ecliptic that has the same declination as the planet. The Sun has one parallel position, the other planets have 2 parallel positions.
\par If a planet's declination where +10 degrees, the parallel positions would be approximately 25 degrees 53 minutes Aries and 4 degrees 7 minutes Virgo. 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {Zodiac Contra-parallels
\par }{\b0 A planet's longitude and latitude is first used to calculate its declination.
\par The contra-parallel position of the planet is that point on the ecliptic that has the opposite declination as the planet. The Sun and other planets all have 2 contra-parallel positions.
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 If a planet's declination where +10 degrees, take the opposite value of -10 degrees. The contra-parallel positions would be approx
imately 25 degrees 53 minutes Libra and 4 degrees 7 minutes Pisces.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {Zodiac Parallels and Contra-parallels when planet's declination is out of bounds
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 When a planet's declination exceeds the Sun's maximum or minimum declination (approximately +23 degrees 27
 minutes and -23 degrees 27 minutes) the excess declination is subtracted from 23 degrees 27 minutes.
\par If for example, a planet's declination where +24 degrees and 30 minutes, this value exceeds the obliquity of the ecliptic by 1 degree 3 minutes. The plane
t's declination would then be taken as +22 degrees 24 minutes to calculate parallels and contra-parallels.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {Mundane Parallels
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 The parallel position of a planet or point is its mirror image about the RAMC and RAIC axis.
\par For example, if the RAMC is 0 degrees and Sun's RA is 10 degrees, the Sun's parallel position would be 350 degrees in RA.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {Mundane Contra-parallels
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 The contra-parallel position of a planet or point is its mirror image about the OAASC and the OADSC axis. For example, if the OAASC is 90 degrees
 and the Moon's RA is 70 degrees, the Moon's contra-parallel position would be 110 degrees in RA.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {How Aspects are handled in Zodiac Directions
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 The aspect value is first added to (or subtracted from) the promittors longitude (or the significators longitude) and then the aspect point is converted to Right Ascension.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {How Aspects are handled in Mundane Directions
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 The promittors (or significators) longitude position is first converted to Right Ascension and then the aspect value is added to (or subtracted from
) the planets RA to produce the aspect point in RA.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {Bianchini Latitude Option
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 This option is only applicable for the zodiac placidus semi-arc and the zodiac regiomontanus calculation methods when the Prom PL -> Sig SL aspect option is selected.
\par The Bianchini Latitude Circle is used to calculate the aspect of promittor latitude and the aspect of significator latitude.
\par Aspect angles are measured along this great circle starting from the planet's ecliptic longitude and ecliptic latitude position.
\par 
\par When the aspect angle is zero the circle passes through the planet's true position and the bianchini latitude is the planet's latitude.
\par When the aspect angle is 90 degrees and 270 degrees the circle crosses the ecliptic and the bianchini latitude is zero.
\par When the aspect angle is 180 degrees the circle passes through the planets opposite position on the celestial sphere and the bianchini latitude is the opposite sign of the planet's latitude.
\par 
\par Aspects of planet at 0 degrees Aries with latitude + 5 degrees.
\par When aspect angle = 0 degrees, aspect point = 0 Aries, aspect latitude = + 5 degrees.
\par When aspect Angle = 90 degrees, aspect point = 0 Cancer, aspect latitude = 0 degrees.
\par When aspect Angle = 180 degrees, aspect point = 0 Libra, aspect latitude = - 5 degrees.
\par When aspect angle = 270 degrees, aspect point = 0 Capricorn, aspect latitude = 0 degrees.
\par 
\par The formula for calculating the Bianchini latitude is
\par Aspect's Ecliptic Latitude = arctan( tan(B) x cos(A) )
\par where B = Planet's ecliptic latitude
\par where A = Aspect value (0 to 360)
\par 
\par In the list of directions, you will get
\par sin/dex Aspect Prom BL d/c => Sig SL
\par Prom PL d/c => sin/dex Aspect Sig BL
\par where BL means bianchini latitude.
\par where PL means promittor uses its own latitude
\par where SL means significator uses its own latitude
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {
\par Date
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 While a direction date may correspond closely to an event date, some astrologers allow up to 30 days either side of the primary direction date.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {Interpretation
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 There are no standard interpretations that are applicable to primary directions. The nature
 and condition (ie. benefic or malefic, house position, strength, affliction etc) of the promittor and the significator should be considered before formulating an interpretation.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {Custom Settings
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 Click Settings > Custom Settings to open the Custom Settings dialog box.
\par Use this feature to save or get a previously saved group of settings.
\par You could for example turn on a selection of planets and stars and save those settings under custom settings using a description of your choosing.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {The Technique
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 The system 
of Primary Directions is based on the principle that a one-degree movement in Right Ascension over the Meridian (due to the diurnal rotation of the Earth on its axis) is equivalent to one year of life.
\par 
\par 1440 minutes (24 hours) of sidereal time = 360 degrees of RA.
\par 360 minutes (6 hours) of sidereal time = 90 degrees of RA = 90 years of life.
\par 60 minutes (1 hour) of sidereal time = 15 degrees of RA = 15 years of life.
\par 40 minutes of sidereal time = 10 degrees of RA = 10 years of life.
\par 4 minutes of sidereal time = 1 degree of RA = 1 year of life.
\par 1 minute of sidereal time = 15 minutes of RA = 3 months of life.
\par 1 second of sidereal time = 15 seconds of RA = 1 day 12 hours of life.
\par 
\par The natal planet zodiac positions are taken from the time of birth. These fixed zodiac positions are directed by diurnal motion, (not by calculating new planetary positions as used in secondary progressions).
\par 
\par Note: Directing the Ascendant or Midheaven to a planet or directing a planet to the Ascendant or Midheaven should be used with caution as a 4 minute uncertainty in the birth time can result in a 1 year uncertainty in primary direction event timing.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {Calculation Methods
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1\cf2 Zodiac Placidus Semi-Arc
\par }{\f1 Also known as Placidus Classic, Ptolemy.
\par This method is based on the publication "Tools and Techniques of the Medieval Astrologer, Book Two, Astrological Prediction by Direction and the Subdivision of the Signs" by Robert E Zoller.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1\cf2 Zodiac Regiomontanus under the pole
\par }{\f1 This method is based on the formulas published by Rique Pottenger and Mark Pottenger.
\par http://www.ccrsdodona.org/m_dilemma/1987/gem/primdir.html
\par http://www.ccrsdodona.org/m_dilemma/1987/sag/primary.html
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf2 Mundane Placidus Semi-Arc
\par }{\f1 This method is based on the publication "Primary Directions - A Primer of Calculation" by Bob Makransky. www.dearbrutus.com
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf2 Mundane Regiomontanus under the pole
\par }{\f1 This method is based on the publication "primary Directions - A Primer of Calculation" by Bob Makransky. www.dearbrutus.com
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {Speculum
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 To open the speculum dialog box, click File > Speculum.
\par 
\par To calculate a speculum:
\par 
\par Select Method
\par * Placidus Semi-arc zero latitude
\par * Placidus Semi-Arc with latitude meaning each planet uses its own latitude
\par * Regiomontanus zero latitude
\par * Regiomontanus with latitude meaning each planet uses its own latitude
\par 
\par Select Text Option
\par * Plain text
\par * Rich text - normally selected
\par 
\par Click the Planets tab and select the planets and extra points you wish to use.
\par 
\par Click the Stars tab and select the stars you wish to use.
\par An * will appear beside those stars which are brighter than selected magnitude and also fall within the selected latitude limit. Use this feature to identify bright stars close to the ecliptic.
\par Click the Select BS to select only the * bright stars and de-select all others.
\par 
\par Click the Speculum tab and click the Calculate button.
\par 
\par To print the speculum, click the Print label or click File > Print from the menu.
\par To copy the speculum, click the Copy label or click Edit > Copy from the menu.
\par If you want to paste the speculum into an email, use plain text.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {Rectify Chart Feature
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 Click File > Rectify Chart to open the Rectify Chart dialog box.
\par Select H (hours), M (minutes) or S (seconds) and click + to advance the birth time or }{\f16 -}{\f1  to retard the birth time.
\par 
\par Tick the Auto-Calculate Directions check box if you want the list of directions to refresh each time you adjust the birth time.
\par 
\par When the Show Asc and Mc Aspects check box is ticked, only aspects involving the Asc and Mc will appear in the list of results.
\par 
\par When the Show Asc and Mc aspects check box is unchecked all aspects will appear.
\par 
\par Tick the Don't Sort Aspect List check box to prevent the list resorting when adjusting the birth time.
\par 
\par When rectifying the birth time, look for directions involving the Ascendant or Midheaven, because the position of these points will change 
the most when altering the birth time. Take an event that had a major impact in the person's life and see if any primary directions involving the angles occurred around that date. Fine-tune the birth time to get a better match. If more than one event is u
sed, you may need to settle on a time that fits best.
\par Rectifying a chart can be challenging, even for an experienced astrologer.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {Zodiac Placidus Semi-Arc Calculation Formula
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 Prom means Promittor
\par Sig means Significator
\par RA means Right Ascension
\par OA means Oblique Ascension
\par UMD means Upper Meridian Distance
\par LMD means Lower Meridian Distance
\par DSA means Diurnal Semi Arc
\par NSA means Nocturnal Semi Arc
\par 
\par 1. Calculate Significator of Right Circle
\par SORC = PromRA - SigRA
\par If SORC < 0 then add 360
\par 
\par 2. Calculate Significator of Region
\par SOR = PromOA - SigOA
\par If SOR < 0 then add 360
\par 
\par 3. Calculate Difference
\par }\pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {DIFF = ABS(SORC - SOR)
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 If DIFF > 180 then subtract 360
\par 
\par 4. Calculate Proportional Part
\par }\pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {
\par If the significator falls in the upper left or upper right quadrant (ie above the horizon) then
\par PP = DIFF X SigUMD / SigDSA
\par 
\par If the significator falls in the lower left or lower right quadrant (ie below the horizon) then
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 PP = DIFF X SigLMD / SigNSA
\par 
\par 5. Calculate Arc of direction
\par 
\par If the significator falls in the upper left or lower left quadrants (left half) and SORC < SOR then
\par Arc = SORC + PP
\par 
\par If the significator falls in the upper left or lower left quadrants (left half) and SORC > SOR then
\par Arc = SORC - PP
\par 
\par If the significator falls in the upper right or the lower right quadrants (right half) and SORC < SOR then
\par Arc = SORC - PP
\par 
\par If the significator falls in the upper right or the lower right quadrants (right half) and SORC > SOR then
\par Arc = SORC + PP
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {Zodiac Placidus Semi-Arc Calculation Example
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang3081\cgrid {\f1 We will use Madonna's chart here.
\par Find the arc of Sun directed to the conjunction of Venus.
\par Using the Sun's own latitude and Venus's own latitude.
\par From the values listed in the speculum:
\par 
\par SORC = PromRA - SigRA
\par = SunRA - VenusRA
\par = 145.448423 - 122.810601
\par = 22.637822
\par 
\par SOR = PromOA - SigOA
\par = SunOA - VenusOA
\par = 131.907219 - 102.132551
\par = 29.774668
\par 
\par DIFF = ABS(SORC - SOR)
\par = Absolute Value (22.637822 - 29.774668)
\par = 7.136846
\par 
\par The significator Venus falls in the upper left quadrant (above horizon) so
\par PP = DIFF X SigUMD / SigDSA
\par = 7.136846 X 62.325219 / 110.678050
\par = 4.018913
\par 
\par The significator falls in the upper left quadrant (left half) and SORC is less than SOR so
\par Arc = SORC + PP
\par = 22.637822 - 4.018913
\par = 26.656735
\par = 26 degs 39 mins 24 secs
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\lang3081\cgrid {References
